import ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.StandardFormGenerator

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.19 — the generator matrix of a standard-form parity check

*(N&C §10.4.1, p. 447.)*

For H=[A|I], corresponding generator matrix is G=[I;-A].

## What the exercise asks, and what is proved

N&C present a linear code either as the *kernel* of a parity-check matrix `H` (`H x = 0`, eq.
(10.57)) or as the *column span* of a generator matrix `G` (the range of `x ↦ G x`). "The
corresponding generator matrix is `G`" therefore means: *the code generated by `G` equals the code
with parity check `H`*, i.e. `range (x ↦ G x) = ker (z ↦ H z)`. This is exactly what is proved.

The formalisation is in `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/StandardFormGenerator.lean`, alongside
the classical-coding development (`LinearCode`, `RepetitionCode`, `GeneratorMatrix`, `DualCode`).

## Declarations

All in `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/StandardFormGenerator.lean`, in namespace `Matrix`, over
an
arbitrary commutative ring `R` (N&C's binary code is `R = ZMod 2`). The `n = k + (n−k)` code
coordinates are modelled by the sum type `κ ⊕ μ`, with `κ` the `k` message positions, `μ` the
`n − k` check positions, and `A : Matrix μ κ R`:

* `Matrix.stdParityCheck A` — **the standard-form parity-check matrix** `H = [A ∣ I_{n-k}]`
  (`Matrix.fromCols A 1 : Matrix μ (κ ⊕ μ) R`).
* `Matrix.stdGenerator A` — **the corresponding generator matrix** `G = [I_k ; −A]`
  (`Matrix.fromRows 1 (-A) : Matrix (κ ⊕ μ) κ R`), N&C eq. (10.59).
* `Matrix.stdParityCheck_mulVec`, `Matrix.stdGenerator_mulVec` — the block form of the two encoding
  maps.
* `Matrix.stdParityCheck_mul_stdGenerator` — **`H · G = 0`**, the algebraic core
  `A · I + I · (−A) = A − A = 0` (the standard-form case of Exercise 10.18).
* `Matrix.stdGenerator_mulVecLin_injective` — the columns of `G` are linearly independent (`G` has
  full column rank `k`), so `G` is a genuine `[n, k]` generator matrix.
* `Matrix.range_mulVecLin_stdGenerator` — **the exercise itself**:
  `range (stdGenerator A).mulVecLin = ker (stdParityCheck A).mulVecLin`, i.e. `G = [I ; −A]` is a
  generator matrix for the code with parity check `H = [A ∣ I]`.

## Faithfulness

* **Statement.** "`G` is the corresponding generator matrix" is captured exactly as equality of the
  two presentations of the code — the column span of `G` and the kernel of `H`
  (`range_mulVecLin_stdGenerator`), phrased with the same raw `LinearMap.range`/`LinearMap.ker` of
  `Matrix.mulVecLin` used throughout the surrounding development (`Matrix.range_mulVecLin_addCol`,
  `LinearCode.dual_ker_mulVecLin`). Nothing is weakened.
* **`−A`, and generality.** N&C note that `G = [I ; −A]` "also holds for linear codes over more
  general fields than `Z₂`". The formalisation keeps the genuine negation `−A` and proves the result
  over an arbitrary commutative ring `R` — the honest generalisation N&C point to, and strictly more
  general (the argument never divides). Over `R = ZMod 2` the book's `−A = A` is recovered.
-/
